ss. |\n55|| Check present but uncalled from production | The guard is implemented and unit-tested by direct call; no production path invokes it. | Drive the real entry point — launch, materialize, resolve, publish — and name the production call site. |\n56|| Bypass path around the check | One entry point enforces; a second, older, or retry/renewal path does not. | Exercise every entry point that reaches the protected state, including retry, renewal, resume, and recovery, and every delivery surface that carries the rule — a contract clause reaching one context profile and not the other is this shape. |\n57|| Capability claim that does not reproduce | A capability is declared absent or present to make a flaky check go away. | Re-run the claim enough times to state a real rate, or report unknown. |\n58|\n59|## Derive Completeness From An Independent Source\n60|\n61|Completeness is derived from the pinned source or generated artifact, never\n62|enumerated by hand. When a registry, schema set, or declared scope defines the\n63|members, derive the expected set from that source and fail when any member lacks\n64|enforcement. This rule comes from five consecutive review rounds that each\n65|closed exactly the hand-picked members named by the previous reviewer; the next\n66|unlisted member defeated every list.\n67|\n68|A completeness test that reads the implementation's own table is circular and\n69|can never detect a missing entry: removing the entry also removes it from the\n70|test's expected set. Derive the expectation from the normative source of truth,\n71|not from the code under test. This distinction was what let the next Story's\n72|round-1 review demand a source-derived completeness test instead of accepting\n73|the producer's two selected cases.\n74|\n75|## Prove Exact Bounds At The Production Entry\n76|\n77|Prove every bound in both directions at the production entry: accept at the\n78|limit and refuse past the limit. Character bounds count characters rather than